grid-columns: 1
vars: {
  d2-config: {
    layout-engine: elk
    theme-id: 300
}}

#t1 -- t2: specification
# literate: { a1: vim; a2: sh; b1: d2; b2: md; a* -> b*: generates; b* -> *: instructs; a* -> md: bootstrap}
region: {
  label: "PLANE REGION"
  plane: {
    d1; s2
  }
t2: { 
  terminal -- literate spec: invoked
  spec -- spec: resolve macros
  spec -- script
  script: potential capability (whats possible)
  terminal -- potential capability (whats possible)
  spec <- potential capability (whats possible)
  spec -> script: realised capability (whats conceivable)
  spec <- script: self-actualise capability (achieved)
}
}
notes: |md
 
     ## title
     the *head* command from initial object 
     is at top of file,

     'head' will always be 
     'less than or equal'
     to the file. 

     the tail operation
     this allows a dual operation that has less than relationsthat
     cohere with head.

     So the tail has a preorder,
     and the head that remains valid up to an operation leavingwith
     a terminal object.

     This means the head and tail operation 
     are preserved.
    
     In order to maintain this operation, 
     both head and tail with use the +
     operation so that the morphism to the boundary position can compose. 

     Add the *primitive operation* 'concat'.

     These operations are abstract enough toe be like 
     'head or tail' in functional programming, 
 
     however, category theory allows you to more naturally define all operations 
     as closed symmetric monoidial category.

     need to verify
|

desired capabilities: {
  grid-columns: 2
    title: |md 
      ### Literate Capabilities
    |

    test: |md "" |
    SPEED OF THOUGHT: |md

    **Capability: Document Explainations at the speed of thought**

    - use fragments of source files (markdown, code, etc) to be easily added as nodes to a diagram

    - actualise diagrams in markdowns from within vim
      render d2 codeblock and add as an image block 

    |

    LITERATE SPEC: |md 
    **Capability: Literate Specification**

    - a system to define a catalogue of coherent literate objects that can be composed


    literate goals: 

    - allow natural writing workflow to map realisation to actualisation 

    
    communication capabilities: describe an action and what it does, 
    this gets map to providing an example, 

    and is documented 
    such that it can be played with 
    |
  
  }
